id "296eff6c-9206-5823-bb93-0f28b35e920f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 3" :db/id 17592186085761} {:answer/id #uuid "5b320f84-2893-5a99-b959-4cf809c16c6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186085762} {:answer/id #uuid "6e876cfd-ddd5-5cc2-a627-8112dec7ef0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id 17592186085763}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f48246f-51c4-5662-886c-8db6c571a5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186085758}] :question/id #uuid "8fc376c7-6855-5bb9-8474-018bf5ce2776" :question/math-academy-id "q-4629" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{3x^{2} - 12x}{x^{2} - 2x - 3}$."}] [{:db/id 17592186088186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3a4c00-8634-58fc-90d6-3294ac3c3371" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186327471} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id 17592186328172} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c362b-0329-5e15-9f0d-4bb67c042f5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/id 17592186328331} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b8294a-f4a8-5a82-a733-0419b620a731" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,x=0,x=3" :db/id 17592186328625} {:answer/id #uuid "c933dda2-ec84-5062-ac4e-5f530086befd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id 17592186328702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id 17592186328172} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8cee54-9108-570e-b47f-cbdac909560a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186088187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has 