db/id 17592186203510}] :question/id #uuid "6bda3cab-2f65-5bfd-a054-8baee9c84dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288058" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n1. $4x - 2 = 4x + 3$\n2. $3x + 4 = 3x + 4$\n3. $5x = 2x + 6$"}] [{:db/id 17592186233061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62ec00bb-a7dc-5ae8-ae64-a716f57687a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102106" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186233151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22ff9dcd-4fa1-5c0a-bb5b-2eec3be880ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-112591" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186234087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2832c750-5223-5055-a543-69f3bfc7bf6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48063" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id 17592186234209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ff428-058e-5197-af7a-fcbe7f3d6387" :question/math-academy-id "q-66785" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\nII. - $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\nIII. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](images/56/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186234403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9857052-b60b-5b90-a22b-cd84e32dabbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](images/43/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186240783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cfd059-85bf-5a42-99f7-811485a4979e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186238504} {:answer/id #uuid "508f5c20-45d7-5202-90ab-5275c4e71dea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186238751} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1104d6-594d-5913-9b22-0cd4f65dd066" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186238844} {:answer/id #uuid "a55adbe4-dfc8-5852-b3c2-e09b91ff35b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186239491} {:answer/id #uuid "fa180e81-a0f8-58ae-befc-5ded12bd0c91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id 17592186240267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55adbe4-dfc8-5852-b3c2-e09b91ff35b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186239491} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b7429d8-0653-5e56-8af7-5e36a2965088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2df95855-c5ea-57bc-9c0a-5ac2a69badf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104020" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c351342c55982f064955a311f9ea94220d58675e85c2e3514a12a8798f03df0.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186240784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a7b2e0-393c-52f9-9f69-6d2bc145fd89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186238084} {:answer/id #uuid "5a801021-53a0-5fe6-a9d2-ac3139f5e29f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id 17592186238837} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2fbbab-fc41-586d-bc31-f9711056f09a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186239987} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f563e-209c-5b0a-9b22-7def371eed8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186240016} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0cccfd-41bb-52ad-918d-e36b5148b3ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id 17592186240140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e03f563e-209c-5b0a-9b22-7def371eed8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186240016} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f7c316-c3c2-564d-8578-a6b33a465102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "597a789c-a68c-549f-b2b0-c1130bde6299" :question/math-academy-id "q-104023" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ee061e9348ada782791fca64a55923834dc87393518e9ec41c49698bfb2f92e.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186247364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d506aa-841e-5c5b-a1bf-5ac09f60017e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186244603} {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186244940} {:answer/id #uuid "65c64b8c-1872-5671-805d-e70b0c6511d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186245211} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1ce093-9dd6-5b3f-aef8-928fca896ee6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186245542} {:answer/id #uuid "ede102f7-ea3c-59b6-a3e1-e4bb8edb3ff1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id 17592186246415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186244940} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83b8a24-f8de-5be9-ab19-5920066dd724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113525" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186247371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b06798-2160-5123-adeb-8fac4e25409f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id 17592186244493} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4901-52db-5ea7-b778-613db328f9ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id 17592186244797} {:answer/id #uuid "95e4ede5-330a-5d5d-b519-47ca590e8dbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id 17592186245627} {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186246321} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4b0856-0eaf-556c-a63b-bf6bfda2eaae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id 17592186246421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186246321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5440fc-dc76-5e79-91b4-ff690a389de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186262919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23779567-a8ad-53b9-bba7-20eb9434d446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id 17592186260186} {:answer/id #uuid "252ff177-ca33-56d5-90df-65c900fa8710" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ only" :db/id 17592186260196} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0fd5bb-e524-591c-95eb-dd9ac92e0ae7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id 17592186260410} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3682d9-c2f8-516c-9549-f226b96cd4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id 17592186261080} {:answer/id #uuid "cfca039c-c8d9-5aa9-b3cf-495e5bcbfed8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id 17592186261709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23779567-a8ad-53b9-bba7-20eb9434d446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id 17592186260186} :answer-field/id #uuid "9694ecaf-94de-5d7f-a9c8-24cf2ab3c726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48261b12-c86c-55b1-9e2a-f38acbfa450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128517" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3472f3af-6fdf-584a-814a-6ecb7e937778" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$ only" :db/id 17592186263460} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe7a9e0-5fc1-5805-b935-6a06de4477cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$  only" :db/id 17592186263966} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f55d58-3424-5ef2-8a1f-da0ba8536bd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186264379} {:answer/id #uuid "ac46a045-085c-5912-a741-16ccb2b1102a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $-5$ only" :db/id 17592186264480} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d1d69-fac7-5c39-83ff-f1d420f126d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $6$ only" :db/id 17592186264926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3472f3af-6fdf-584a-814a-6ecb7e937778" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$ only" :db/id 17592186263460} :answer-field/id #uuid "8780a347-d9fa-54e7-9af1-9bb48bd7be2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50033102-58e0-5acc-81a2-81bf1717d471" :question/math-academy-id "q-130575" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $-48$, find all the possible values of $z.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & z \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & z \\\\ z & 1\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ z & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3+z)-(2-{z}^{2})+5(-2-3z) \\\\  & =6+2z-2+{z}^{2}-10-15z \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-13z-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-48,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-13z-6 & =-48 \\\\ {z}^{2}-13z+42 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{-(-13)\\pm \\sqrt{{13}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 42}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm \\sqrt{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm 1}{2} \\\\  & =6,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=6$ and $z=7.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d38c9cc-ca7f-591e-8a8d-b51db0107fb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186263380} {:answer/id #uuid "e51b3361-87bb-58aa-8055-e13c58174671" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ only" :db/id 17592186264992} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c47758-325d-5cfc-acdb-c862f1f74e21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186265025} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5b351-94ef-5b84-a4b9-ea4263b1e26b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ and $-1$ only" :db/id 17592186265067} {:answer/id #uuid "f13d3fa2-d171-577a-a448-dd5b107fd44b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $-3$ only" :db/id 17592186265119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece5b351-94ef-5b84-a4b9-ea4263b1e26b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ and $-1$ only" :db/id 17592186265067} :answer-field/id #uuid "da945efe-22fe-5afe-9a1b-b80334eb4404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c642acf-da11-5fe2-a467-6e32aaff486e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130580" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 3 \\\\ 5 & x & 7 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $22$, find all the possible values of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 3 \\\\ 5 & x & 7 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =x|\\begin{aligned}x & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2x|\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}5 & x \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x(x-14)-2x(5-21)+3(10-3x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-14x+32x+30-9x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9x+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $22,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9x+30 & =22 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 8}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-8,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-8$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21390a7b-0a54-5e9a-9011-c7deff139c41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ only" :db/id 17592186263297} {:answer/id #uuid "91d75f72-1a96-5644-a529-caf7355ab1bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id 17592186264249} {:answer/id #uuid "970c8319-2067-5d80-a590-021621a09030" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186264292} {:answer/id #uuid "f080186a-d1c4-58fa-9d1f-db014dd38fcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ only" :db/id 17592186265109} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1c675c-a294-5ee2-a8ca-549d874e48c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10$ only" :db/id 17592186265208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91d75f72-1a96-5644-a529-caf7355ab1bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id 17592186264249} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69f31fe-6848-54a7-8e0e-0e1258ee7ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fee93468-4926-51f6-9486-b49c687c7f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130626" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $p$ given that the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}6p & -8 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3p)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6p \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6p-8)+3p(-2+8)+4(2-6p) \\\\  & =6p-8+18p+8-24p \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix is singular for all values of $p.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186287821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04828dd4-f030-5633-b9ab-2ca90b202225" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $1$ only" :db/id 17592186284827} {:answer/id #uuid "10872bea-02dc-53b8-8a81-d21f0443ee2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $1$ only" :db/id 17592186284932} {:answer/id #uuid "28dc2ce6-543c-571a-a921-bb628ebdeb90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id 17592186285145} {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "827cdff1-9d64-55e9-b43a-8f9a6a5b14e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id 17592186285984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id 17592186285398} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf370fc0-22a1-5807-80d7-314f76c5d442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "627e7a3d-46e0-5ce8-b4f0-d14bd806cb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-18256" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}]$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}| & =4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ y & -5\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-3y & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-3y & -2 \\\\ 2 & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (10-y)-2\\cdot (15y-2)+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =40-4y-30y+4-9{y}^{2}+12 \\\\  & =-9{y}^{2}-34y+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $81,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{y}^{2}-34y+56 & =81 \\\\ -9{y}^{2}-34y-25 & =0 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+34y+25 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{{34}^{2}-4\\cdot 9\\cdot 25}}{2\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{256}}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm 16}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-17\\pm 8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186287825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121a8609-4bb7-5c67-9bdc-2801df41173e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $4$ only" :db/id 17592186284949} {:answer/id #uuid "429a9de6-9e34-5d60-bd99-5ca64acccf3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $5$ only" :db/id 17592186285403} {:answer/id #uuid "77aaca00-7a72-5562-b3c2-49acc2dc1a52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $5$ only" :db/id 17592186285900} {:answer/id #uuid "a7439201-e9e4-57ad-a8e9-5b2518ed22ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ only" :db/id 17592186286313} {:answer/id #uuid "c28c20d4-0f99-5907-b488-8276cbb024db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ only" :db/id 17592186286570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429a9de6-9e34-5d60-bd99-5ca64acccf3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $5$ only" :db/id 17592186285403} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb400fb3-60c2-5a2f-a4e3-20a5f1ab4cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a6ffa24-d6e8-52ac-ac3d-5fab4f24df3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18272" :question/problem "Given that $M=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $det(M)=-23,$ find all the possible values of $p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}p & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ p-3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & p \\\\ p-3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (3p-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3-2{p}^{2}+6p \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+6p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-23,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{p}^{2}+6p-3 & =-23 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}-6p-20 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-3p-10 & =0 \\\\ p & =\\frac{-(-3)\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-10)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186326784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id 17592186324042} {:answer/id #uuid "55df2ba0-f2c6-5604-9a35-63997cf52830" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=5" :db/id 17592186324686} {:answer/id #uuid "cb249387-39bb-5bca-aeb9-bb3d6e5d3f07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186325733} {:answer/id #uuid "e80c846d-e256-503b-bd6e-c02635e34775" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id 17592186326014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0231bbc-1648-5f25-943c-15ee288b5418" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186326090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id 17592186324042} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e143c6-8b95-56da-82eb-2acad7352c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186326431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd01e245-cc8a-5763-b4a7-8579762d92bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-39804" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{3}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-1{)}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x+5{)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-5.$ Therefore, $x=-5$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id 17592186326791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2964b3dc-2d94-539a-946c-a30b2755e2da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id 17592186324304} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cc3a3-58f1-5d26-8673-5d478b6c9cda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186324724} {:answer/id #uuid "73ff3097-7a21-5a24-ac11-6c28413a6ab4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186324935} {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186325221} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e97693-64f6-596d-9b4a-111e079d80af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186325567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186325221} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f771b5-672e-5778-ace6-2b8c22fa7988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186326435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720a20e0-eaeb-515c-b041-a17373ebe5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39931" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{{x}^{2}\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id 17592186326792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186324389} {:answer/id #uuid "b527320d-de25-5e52-98bb-3d68c7423f78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id 17592186325527} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f3c2a-a49e-5897-801c-424300e7c8a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186325808} {:answer/id #uuid "d670e8d0-18bf-5af2-8bdf-5ce021e9152d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186325837} {:answer/id #uuid "f34e9c7e-81fd-5622-b802-8d9eedc6bd83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ only" :db/id 17592186326114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186324389} :answer-field/id #uuid "08795b3d-c8a6-5731-9194-35bf6c829513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186326237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f92ee8b-33fa-5343-9c16-73df2f465b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39934" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{x({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+2x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+8=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(8) \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186329861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097a0abc-e5fd-5fb9-87cc-5feb12a99af5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id 17592186327011} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6bc529-d89b-5eb7-8d47-60d31ea78ba5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3" :db/id 17592186327743} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a8878d-10ae-5348-b9a9-3a79555570b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186328484} {:answer/id #uuid "c659e8b4-0a4b-5891-b59b-eae29924cca4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186328676} {:answer/id #uuid "e396cf90-de23-5177-aa7c-f3b3f49336bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186328929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "097a0abc-e5fd-5fb9-87cc-5feb12a99af5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id 17592186327011} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3e238cc-c08e-5e81-9ee9-5193fc6a3f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "172d7de1-0c8f-50b5-8e69-09efd0164cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48087" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f53373ebbb7e812471001388ba88122afbde33cd3317c454466168b3e633b58.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=2$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186339110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ae43d1-4fec-5e37-8920-14fdf8f6c77d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id 17592186336245} {:answer/id #uuid "464aa712-2203-5c71-852e-42121f806af6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ only" :db/id 17592186336685} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb7b868-b5d8-579b-adf6-6962389cbae6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id 17592186337020} {:answer/id #uuid "84b9fe17-3141-5ba4-b930-81eb79ca211c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id 17592186337228} {:answer/id #uuid "971e49c0-6fdc-5a97-b4f7-0171b41fa764" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$  and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186337407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb7b868-b5d8-579b-adf6-6962389cbae6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id 17592186337020} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db519c2-9383-5593-acf5-9c05c003290b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c0801c9-8ff6-5c1f-a200-5a0877707694" :question/math-academy-id "q-72410" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2xy={x}^{2}+8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186339115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df1c7eb-8340-5ae5-bb12-5c1eac211a43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and  $(-2,4)$ only" :db/id 17592186336164} {:answer/id #uuid "3661bffb-2951-513d-a543-ac65bfdf1f97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ and  $(-4,1)$ only" :db/id 17592186336537} {:answer/id #uuid "423b1542-232d-58c0-9337-ae84e6e0aa74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and  $(4,-4)$ only" :db/id 17592186336658} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e95c16-5a95-5943-85ba-54d73ac6ae5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only" :db/id 17592186337541} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fad17d-f3ef-5003-b21c-026ac1013257" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ only" :db/id 17592186337660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6e95c16-5a95-5943-85ba-54d73ac6ae5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only" :db/id 17592186337541} :answer-field/id #uuid "61738848-0a31-5b0d-adc3-57da8b8e226b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5577e429-0d55-5dd4-b588-49ac727edd6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72440" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+2xy=-5y+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)j \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252db0d7-6b72-5535-bc72-42087bd9c168" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id 17592186345686} {:answer/id #uuid "3c61c17f-df35-5231-8f8c-9855ef44fbff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186345862} {:answer/id #uuid "493990c6-7871-5af7-9742-efe497570105" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186345976} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id 17592186346424} {:answer/id #uuid "e86bd07f-57d6-5d65-989c-5c3ebae8b75b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=0" :db/id 17592186347400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id 17592186346424} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a0cf42-b725-56a9-a31d-6affed13eb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45255a84-5494-574a-bd9f-880b4b37a48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80669" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186345784} {:answer/id #uuid "4aeb91f0-cd53-5d4d-886c-fb0fd62c216e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0" :db/id 17592186345993} {:answer/id #uuid "72812abe-a3ca-5de6-b805-36c4e38ad777" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id 17592186346366} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe36be7-496a-56d8-8d43-bee44a476d95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id 17592186346750} {:answer/id #uuid "e47b15d6-db10-5d9f-9cc1-04b2bdf4db96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id 17592186347364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186345784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9c99d4-e1f0-5d10-83b9-846a389a4e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e336039-4f92-550b-b76c-a2a62662e884" :question/math-academy-id "q-80676" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+4(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e616195-c20c-5ce6-a774-a9d27760ce9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186345468} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbadb36-36cf-57bb-b77c-60cf2f937db8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=-2" :db/id 17592186346153} {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186346626} {:answer/id #uuid "a473e00b-5a25-5f34-8225-eb11ceb36777" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186346779} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e0eef-d46c-5409-9e99-5a2584e7abcd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id 17592186347339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186346626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2918e306-8491-5fc4-93b6-c33073f50077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dad69b3-29eb-58b2-9a73-eefa5740f30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80691" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id 17592186348437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186345870} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b5cb8-178a-5fd6-856c-24bea81fccc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186346879} {:answer/id #uuid "c858a68d-354f-5009-8590-cab42e410104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186347095} {:answer/id #uuid "d8905902-e64e-5add-b785-c52f97aa4e79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186347258} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d09f7-625f-5ed9-981b-246d07b3adc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186347260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186345870} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f66b65f-a57a-5bd8-9c0c-795c2010f044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186362340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24569432-1366-5b0e-a6fa-35329602b43d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186360990} {:answer/id #uuid "405ba37d-88bc-588c-be97-45c88986f4c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ only" :db/id 17592186361126} {:answer/id #uuid "822d6e62-ce1f-5081-8c99-18fcb72e9a13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id 17592186361416} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0b9dec-a477-56d5-8568-22939d39be14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $-3$ only" :db/id 17592186361539} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c897ad-8b3e-52b7-83a5-70beda9d09d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ only" :db/id 17592186361665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24569432-1366-5b0e-a6fa-35329602b43d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186360990} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe2b409-187a-57b6-9b05-5d49a74a922b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e554c090-e3bc-5a93-90b4-9f79a0997b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-9911" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}k & -k \\\\ k & k\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}|+k|\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({k}^{2}+{k}^{2})+(k+5k)+k(k-5k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k+k\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-4{k}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{k}^{2}+6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{k}^{2}+6k & =0 \\\\ k(-2k+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186373757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8afc3ae9-00f3-516a-bbcb-c44682d96676" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id 17592186373759} {:answer/id #uuid "81d8dc6b-68e6-5833-9743-e2dd9a57e9e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186373760} {:answer/id #uuid "894083b9-b198-57bd-991b-77b81ac6aedf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ only" :db/id 17592186373761} {:answer/id #uuid "a0589f58-1050-5bd6-9ac8-d8b3fa41a8fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$" :db/id 17592186373762} {:answer/id #uuid "9d22b9c8-42b9-5f43-a01d-96f9e724e330" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ only" :db/id 17592186373763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0589f58-1050-5bd6-9ac8-d8b3fa41a8fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$" :db/id 17592186373762} :answer-field/id #uuid "f40ff009-072c-5417-b244-503451b38228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d9c5851-2645-50db-8b4e-b731824fd7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130225" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2({0}^{2})-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(3y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186393514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85035df-3f18-5f4a-8b7d-4120d3a258fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id 17592186393516} {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id 17592186393517} {:answer/id #uuid "57d3f469-2ccf-551a-b2e0-ffe31d3bca63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id 17592186393518} {:answer/id #uuid "6712e860-a397-555b-b691-57588df29d04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id 17592186393519} {:answer/id #uuid "332bdb7a-e350-5dc0-a978-64f0aa49c697" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id 17592186393520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id 17592186393517} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e4af98-e57b-515a-916f-95959841d208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186393515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75278" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186396107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a272db-6815-53ec-87da-5a281c0b5932" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id 17592186396109} {:answer/id #uuid "81bc4acc-cf96-53dd-9604-97c51e416cf4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186396110} {:answer/id #uuid "d3972294-8bb0-593a-9b73-d4e09973c691" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186396111} {:answer/id #uuid "4649603f-3d59-5567-ab7b-c75a268bb6c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186396112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186396113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186396113} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc4a2be-1828-5c44-9645-c520108c6c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186396237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ffa317-1b68-57e4-8682-58b3f2cf3610" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $2$ only" :db/id 17592186396239} {:answer/id #uuid "43dbf530-ae59-578a-b191-6ba7d2edf47b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id 17592186396240} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c887a0-8c33-5ac9-b8f5-287ee0622dd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $0$ only" :db/id 17592186396241} {:answer/id #uuid "0a134bc9-3211-56a8-ba64-b5d8b1f67691" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$ only" :db/id 17592186396242} {:answer/id #uuid "c63b5ccb-5750-5b73-bc77-0f7d019725ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ only" :db/id 17592186396243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a134bc9-3211-56a8-ba64-b5d8b1f67691" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$ only" :db/id 17592186396242} :answer-field/id #uuid "0631763f-bf07-54e2-9810-4d0b16208a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11af43ea-89bf-58ea-b458-4433226aa764" :question/math-academy-id "q-9913" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix  $[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}| & =k|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k(3k-6)+0+0 \\\\  & =k(3k-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(3k-6) & =0 \\\\ 3k(k-2) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186396578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ba84a1-3d25-5cd1-98bf-f731c2622e1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186396580} {:answer/id #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186396581} {:answer/id #uuid "77df7b4b-c87e-5e29-808b-f38574caacd1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186396582} {:answer/id #uuid "8c127ea3-d160-5664-896d-8df951530b12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id 17592186396583} {:answer/id #uuid "d02a6f82-debb-558b-a876-dc02aab50551" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186396584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186396581} :answer-field/id #uuid "53daf543-087e-50bb-928d-d5b44cae5b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6685bf3-2db8-58de-88bd-a80799c06e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104025" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d342689f9e4a213ac5407ccc132868b51ba218afabda1a871e7ca7b02345a17.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-1$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186396673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186396675} {:answer/id #uuid "c08b5c35-a5d7-5069-b56f-c620adb60e5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186396676} {:answer/id #uuid "86dd1c08-dfcc-5440-8ed6-a1599aed6c51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id 17592186396677} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a54622-a441-5f4f-8a9d-6f28f972e33c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id 17592186396678} {:answer/id #uuid "50484ca8-86cd-5313-806f-14cb83956a21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id 17592186396679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id 17592186396675} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5e1e7b-8e38-5b41-953d-3eb5f39cc12b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e088c5-8b59-5061-af03-e9b40ee3d2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48088" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-3$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a12e93a-5479-5bc8-a172-5f001b81680d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186399797} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56062-f5eb-5d51-97c9-2401cc2382c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id 17592186399798} {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186399799} {:answer/id #uuid "060d6765-1a4c-5cef-9036-baa44df95b64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id 17592186399800} {:answer/id #uuid "0532888d-2587-5d82-aa30-da64f92b3ef7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id 17592186399801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id 17592186399799} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de2cff7-92c8-5c70-9102-18ed3f31a5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9bdca2-12f3-5135-add9-f6cee60b6017" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186399804} {:answer/id #uuid "ea637b56-a1b6-530a-b8ad-0581d7725118" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186399805} {:answer/id #uuid "6caf38b1-8d42-5322-9446-2276f113b03b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id 17592186399806} {:answer/id #uuid "88004c88-6f69-578a-840b-c594bbb79e81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id 17592186399807} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186399808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186399808} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9f7db0-7e00-5308-89b8-2a0dcddb1466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186400338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c84e5b-97a7-5116-a90c-e4f011e87cf0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c52bd-3b80-591c-b1ee-da79ec0a2f5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=\\sqrt{6}$ only" :db/id 17592186400340} {:answer/id #uuid "0be7aabd-bcca-5a8a-a91e-5d1dfb6c9d3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-6$ or $k=6$" :db/id 17592186400341} {:answer/id #uuid "07cf905c-bfd7-597a-b011-0bc724c98f3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-\\sqrt{6}$ only" :db/id 17592186400342} {:answer/id #uuid "1c100f4f-e0f0-5fd4-9658-c15b9ace8765" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id 17592186400343} {:answer/id #uuid "7316d970-6133-533d-8bfb-659fe822cc85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-\\sqrt{3}$ or $k=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186400344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c100f4f-e0f0-5fd4-9658-c15b9ace8765" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id 17592186400343} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47d5cb7-6394-509c-81ff-79c6a1373831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41e53fe4-b06e-5150-a976-5badf29f5d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128461" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨{k}^{2},7,4⟩,$ $b=⟨6,7,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,0,2⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & 7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(7\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)-7(6\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 4)+4(6\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}+0-84 \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}-84 \\\\  & =14({k}^{2}-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 14({k}^{2}-6) & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =6 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d167c86-2957-5856-9829-ed639fe987fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id 17592186400368} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b0080f-3903-56c0-99e9-1c714284d709" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $8$ only" :db/id 17592186400369} {:answer/id #uuid "7b00f40d-f528-5d51-ba17-148c04c5be0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $1$ only" :db/id 17592186400370} {:answer/id #uuid "dc29467d-2e6d-5f49-8946-656bd765b95a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ only" :db/id 17592186400371} {:answer/id #uuid "0545f2be-36f5-50f6-a5cb-3ece6041700e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ and $3$ only" :db/id 17592186400372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4b0080f-3903-56c0-99e9-1c714284d709" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $8$ only" :db/id 17592186400369} :answer-field/id #uuid "53dd3293-7046-5b2d-b374-9c7bbea414c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30a80f14-b653-5ec9-a12d-20d43bb40f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-128506" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $-29$, find all the possible values of $m.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-6|\\begin{aligned}m & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+m|\\begin{aligned}m & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-0)-6(m-0)+m(m-6) \\\\  & =3-6m+{m}^{2}-6m \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-12m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-29,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12m+3 & =-29 \\\\ {m}^{2}-12m+32 & =0 \\\\ m & =\\frac{-(-12)\\pm \\sqrt{{12}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 32}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm 4}{2} \\\\  & =6\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=4$ and $m=8.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a10163a-dceb-5ab4-b904-ba9a054ac615" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$" :db/id 17592186400375} {:answer/id #uuid "83d361ab-691b-507d-aa60-ee6cbedb4a3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3)$ only" :db/id 17592186400376} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad8d842-3217-5d0b-a3d1-f6c4802c29c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-3)$ and $(-3,3)$" :db/id 17592186400377} {:answer/id #uuid "e58ac478-7209-5f45-a6c1-f164d3f25552" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,3)$ only" :db/id 17592186400378} {:answer/id #uuid "bf97e181-9c44-5eb6-9ba0-39838ff1806e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ only" :db/id 17592186400379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d361ab-691b-507d-aa60-ee6cbedb4a3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3)$ only" :db/id 17592186400376} :answer-field/id #uuid "975890c9-a5ad-56b9-8079-273dba5ca847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a633d445-62c8-5864-b7bf-d694cd1cd56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128519" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{y})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3b5cf6-0370-5cde-a9e6-1edabf953fa1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ only" :db/id 17592186400452} {:answer/id #uuid "796d0d95-8bf2-5062-97c0-381c6826adbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186400453} {:answer/id #uuid "930c85b6-cd1d-5f01-b245-1181988c9afd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ only" :db/id 17592186400454} {:answer/id #uuid "03b48f4a-4c69-5175-9b51-45915a00e587" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ only" :db/id 17592186400455} {:answer/id #uuid "36a2c161-410d-5532-af77-2aaed16bed2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186400456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "796d0d95-8bf2-5062-97c0-381c6826adbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id 17592186400453} :answer-field/id #uuid "31717cb2-d9d0-54f4-b700-91183260eebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6851bbc7-80d6-5dc9-8a00-f559624fa12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130217" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id 17592186403475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9ec4597-899c-5f21-a16c-2695e9541e21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ only" :db/id 17592186403477} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24ba4d-0c93-5d33-a565-f106f0e3b765" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1)$ and $(-1,1,1)$" :db/id 17592186403478} {:answer/id #uuid "a1afdd74-60dd-5f9b-bad5-2d5610ad3377" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1)$" :db/id 17592186403479} {:answer/id #uuid "e68b95bf-0642-5f05-aa57-9a8fabde2338" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1,-1)$ only" :db/id 17592186403480} {:answer/id #uuid "15edafbf-4f96-51e2-9aa0-c33dbac930ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id 17592186403481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1afdd74-60dd-5f9b-bad5-2d5610ad3377" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1)$" :db/id 17592186403479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca042fa0-0bbe-58d3-8050-de243a925b8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186403476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c503ae86-f71f-5a21-9c90-e19ce4d410a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71474" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186410576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186410563} {:answer/id #uuid "78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186410565} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23f760-5ac7-5ae1-99ac-01f57b186e7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "ll only" :db/id 17592186410566} {:answer/id #uuid "eef188a5-b79b-535e-a698-3abfd5aa7c90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186410571} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9201b9-0d51-54ab-bcd2-eefc57d9346d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "l only" :db/id 17592186410572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186410565} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186410574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186437021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2a514c-da70-5cc8-95d2-327d72d03570" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-2)$ only" :db/id 17592186436987} {:answer/id #uuid "7067d8af-1731-5b47-8f38-e961d32014a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id 17592186436988} {:answer/id #uuid "8f042e63-ea21-547d-8187-c6464d907f1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/id 17592186436991} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cf6f0f-1cc3-5518-a4d7-359da9629347" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2)$" :db/id 17592186436992} {:answer/id #uuid "fb87d0bd-b556-514b-84e7-e694a530dff2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,-2)$" :db/id 17592186437008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cf6f0f-1cc3-5518-a4d7-359da9629347" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2)$" :db/id 17592186436992} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83f4999-419d-51c4-a803-c6789f4e1b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186437013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d13fce5d-8bd4-547a-afec-720ff5befaa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71470" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$"}]] "items" [] "response_users" [] "attributes" [[10 :db/ident] [11 :db.install/partition] [13 :db.install/attribute] [15 :db/excise] [16 :db.excise/attrs] [17 :db.excise/beforeT] [18 :db.excise/before] [19 :db.alter/attribute] [40 :db/valueType] [41 :db/cardinality] [42 :db/unique] [43 :db/isComponent] [44 :db/index] [45 :db/noHistory] [50 :db/txInstant] [51 :db/fulltext] [52 :db/fn] [62 :db/doc] [65 :db/tupleType] [66 :db/tupleTypes] [67 :db/tupleAttrs] [68 :db/ensure] [69 :db.entity/attrs] [70 :db.entity/preds] [71 :db.attr/preds] [72 :db.tuple/discontinued] [1000 :course-group/courses] [1001 :course-group/title] [1002 :course-group/id] [1003 :course/next] [1004 :course/outcome